# Appendix A: prior-work map from brief v3 (still valid; written for the 2026-09-01 cutoff)

## A5. Prior work map (what is established; all dated <= 2026-09-01)

### A5.1 Closest prior theory (the paper must position against these)
1. **Tian, Chen & Ganguli, ICML 2021 (arXiv 2102.06810), DirectPred.** Linear
   BYOL/SimSiam, eigen-aligned modes; on the invariant parabola
   p_j' = p_j (tau p_j - (1+s^2) p_j^2 - eta). The drive (tau p_j) is
   *quadratic* in the mode near the origin, so with any weight decay eta>0 the
   collapsed point is *linearly stable*; a mode survives only if it starts
   outside the basin p_j > p*_- , and all collapse if eta > tau^2/(4(1+s^2)).
   Covers EMA (tau, beta), predictor lr ratio alpha_p, weight decay; no
   masking, predictor depth/width, isotropic data, symmetric predictor.
   **Reviewers will read any drive-vs-decay claim against this paper.**
2. **Arbel, Terver & Ponce 2026, PEIRA (2605.17671)** and **Ponce et al. 2025
   (2507.01028).** Stop-grad + optimal ridge predictor + norm penalty lambda:
   canonical correlation c survives iff c^2 >= 4 lambda; total collapse is
   stable; EMA stability left open. Equilibrium analysis, no masking/predictor
   scaling.
3. **Ziyin, Lubana, Ueda & Tanaka, ICLR 2023 (2210.00638).** "Stability at the
   origin decides collapse": per-subspace linear criterion (signal vs
   augmentation + weight decay) — but only for contrastive/decorrelation
   losses (no predictor, stop-grad, EMA).
4. **Littwin et al., NeurIPS 2024 (2407.03475).** Deep linear JEPA with
   stop-grad: per-feature ODE w' = lambda w^{3-1/L} - lambda w^3/rho; greedy in
   regression coefficient rho; no weight decay, EMA or masking, so nothing
   collapses.
5. **Wang, Chen, Du & Tian 2021 (2110.04947).** DirectSet/DirectCopy: a feature
   is discarded iff its augmentation variance s^2 > 1/(4 eta) - 1.
Also: Simon et al. ICML 2023 (stepwise SSL: linear growth rate at origin, step
time ~ log(1/init)/rate, modes with non-positive rate never grow); Halvagal et
al. NeurIPS 2023 (predictor eigenvalues as per-mode rate multipliers
lambda(1-lambda); no stop-grad -> all collapse); Tang et al. ICML 2023 and
TD-JEPA 2025 (optimal/fast predictor conserves Phi^T Phi: no growth, no
collapse); Bao 2023 (cosine loss changes the drive/decay balance); Zhuo et al.
ICLR 2023 (rank differential); Wen & Li 2022 (predictor rescues weak
features); Richemond et al. 2023 (edge of orthogonality); Jing et al. ICLR 2022
(dimensional collapse in contrastive SSL).

### A5.2 What is not covered (the gap the paper can fill)

(v4 note: as of 2026-10-18 G1-G4 are partly addressed by arXiv 2610.02344, Section 5.1; re-check each gap against it before claiming it.)
- G1. No *linear-stability* (first-order) per-direction criterion at the
  collapsed point for JEPA/self-distillation. In Tian/Wang/Bao/PEIRA the drive
  is quadratic at the origin. A first-order drive arises if the predictor is
  O(1) at the collapsed point (e.g. predictor not collapsed at init): linearising
  w_i' = p_i c_i wbar_i - (p_i^2 a_i + eta) w_i, wbar' = beta (w - wbar) gives
  survival iff p_i c_i > p_i^2 a_i + eta (independent of beta). This is a
  suggestion recorded in `refs/lit_tools.md`, not a prior result. The paper
  must say which regime it treats and reconcile with Tian's basin picture;
  otherwise its novelty over Tian 2021 is unclear.
- G2. No theory of how predictor **width/depth/scale/initialisation** moves the
  per-direction threshold (prior work: lr ratio, closed-form or optimal
  predictors only).
- G3. No collapse-dynamics theory of the **masking ratio** (prior masking
  theory is about MAE optima/identifiability; useful moment identities:
  Bisulco et al. 2025, Dong et al. NeurIPS 2025 — masked covariance
  (1-m)^2 Sigma + m(1-m) diag/blkdiag Sigma; masked-PLS BBP threshold scaling
  rho^{-1/2}, Gjolbye et al. 2026).
- G4. **EMA** enters only qualitatively ("curriculum", Tian) or is left open
  (PEIRA); scaling rule rho -> rho^kappa under batch scaling (Busbridge et al.
  NeurIPS 2023).
- G5. Prior thresholds assume isotropic/commuting data or optimal predictors;
  none is tested against reachable rank in realistic (I-JEPA-style) training.
- G6. Empirically, no paper ablates EMA/no-EMA or sweeps predictor
  depth/width/lr or mask ratio **against rank** for image JEPAs (I-JEPA
  reports accuracy only; LiDAR reports I-JEPA RankMe rises then falls).

### A5.3 Tools the theory can build on
Small-init deep-linear dynamics (Saxe et al. 2014: mode growth rate ~ signal,
time ~ log(1/eps)/s; Li, Luo & Lyu 2021: near 0 the flow is power iteration
U' = -grad f(0) U, adding weight decay lambda gives growth iff mu_i > lambda;
Jacot et al. 2021: escape time ~ alpha^{-(L-2)} for depth L>2; Atanasov et al.
2022 silent alignment). Weight decay as rank threshold (nuclear-norm identity;
Kobayashi et al. NeurIPS 2024: L2 on a factorised map = nuclear norm on the
product, so the predictor∘encoder singular values are soft-thresholded; Ziyin,
Li & Meng NeurIPS 2022: zero is the global minimum iff ||E[xy]||^2 <=
gamma_u gamma_w; Wang & Jacot 2023). EMA/target networks as two-timescale
systems (Tian 2021; Fellows et al. ICML 2023 for TD). Equations: `refs/lit_tools.md`.

### A5.4 What practice establishes about each heuristic (numbers in `refs/lit_practice.md`)
- Stop-grad: required (SimSiam without it 0.1%; BYOL tau=0 -> 0.3%).
- EMA: BYOL ImageNet top-1 tau=1 (frozen random target) 18.8, 0.999 69.8,
  0.99 72.5, 0.9 68.4; DINO without momentum collapses; data2vec: tau too low
  -> collapse. I-JEPA/DINO/BYOL schedule 0.996->1; V-JEPA 2 constant 0.99925.
- Predictor: removal collapses BYOL/SimSiam; fixed random predictor fails;
  BYOL without EMA needs predictor lr multiplier >= 2 (1x -> 5.5%, 2x -> 62.8%);
  I-JEPA depth 6->12 +2.9 (IN-1%), width 384 better than 1024 (bottleneck);
  SimSiam hidden d/4 more robust.
- Masking: I-JEPA multi-block 54.2 vs random 17.6; target scale peaks at
  (0.15,0.2); 1 target block 9.0 vs 4 blocks 54.2; V-JEPA ~90% ratio best
  ("low coverage -> trivial prediction task").
- Weight decay: I-JEPA ramp 0.04->0.4; BYOL wd=0 unstable; Tian: large eta
  forces collapse.
- Rank: in I-JEPA, RankMe rises early then falls; best accuracy is not at max
  rank (LiDAR, Thilak et al. ICLR 2024).

### A5.5 Conventions to follow for comparability
- Rank: RankMe (Garrido et al. ICML 2023; exp-entropy of normalised singular
  values of the N x K embedding matrix, eps ~ 1e-7, ~25.6k samples, compare
  within a method only); optionally LiDAR (Thilak et al. 2024) for JEPAs;
  sorted log singular-value spectra of the centred embedding covariance on
  held-out data (Jing et al. 2022) at several checkpoints; SimSiam std of
  l2-normalised outputs vs 1/sqrt(d).
- RankMe as published is computed on the singular values of the *raw*
  (uncentred) embedding matrix; a plan that uses centred embeddings must say
  so (centring removes the constant "collapsed" component, which is what a
  JEPA collapse theory needs, but it is not RankMe verbatim).
- Per-direction dynamics are reported in prior theory as: eigenvalues of the
  embedding covariance (or of W W^T) vs training time on a log scale, showing
  stepwise growth (Simon et al. 2023, Fig. 1-2); predictor vs encoder
  eigenvalue alignment and p_j(t) trajectories with the predicted fixed
  points p*_+- (Tian et al. 2021); per-feature amplitude w_i(t) with
  predicted critical times (Littwin et al. 2024). Phase diagrams of collapse
  vs (weight decay, other knob) with the analytic boundary overlaid are the
  natural figure for thresholds (Tian 2021 shows basins in (p, s) plane).
- Quality: frozen linear probe (primary), k-NN monitor; mean +- std over >= 3
  seeds. At small scale, logistic regression on frozen, pooled features is
  an accepted cheap probe (simple-ijepa: sklearn LogisticRegression, STL-10);
  solo-learn trains an online/offline linear head with SGD on frozen
  features. Report which probe and its training budget.
- Small-scale benchmarks used by theory papers: CIFAR-10/100, STL-10
  (ResNet-18), ImageNet-100 / ImageNette (ViT-T/S). Expect low absolute I-JEPA
  numbers at small scale (ViT-T 100 ep IN-1K linear 22.1%, C-JEPA Table 9);
  reference baselines: solo-learn BYOL/SimSiam numbers.
- Heuristic grids that mirror practice: EMA tau in {0, 0.9, 0.99, 0.996,
  0.999, 1}; predictor depth/width/lr multiplier; mask ratio / target scale /
  number of blocks; weight decay {0, fixed, ramp}; report collapse as phase
  diagrams of measured rank vs predicted boundary.
- Reusable MIT-licensed code: lightly (I-JEPA modules), galilai-group/
  stable-pretraining (RankMe/LiDAR, I-JEPA benchmarks), solo-learn;
  facebookresearch/ijepa is CC BY-NC (research use OK; state it). Pin
  versions released <= 2026-09-01.

